                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_59\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit adds a `MainActivity.onDestroy()` override that calls\n`DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this)`, and adds a new\n`destroy(Context)` method to `DownloadManagerInterface` that unregisters\n`downloadCompletionReceiver` and clears the singleton instance. This shows the\nbug was: `DownloadManagerInterface` registers a `BroadcastReceiver` via\n`context.registerReceiver(downloadCompletionReceiver, filter)` but that\nreceiver was never unregistered anywhere, causing a receiver/resource leak.\n\n`candidate_59` is where this problem was introduced. Before this commit,\n`MainActivity` had its own broadcast-receiver handling for download\ncompletion, complete with correct lifecycle management:\n`registerReceiver()` in `onCreate()` and a matching\n`unregisterReceiver()` call inside an `onDestroy()` override.\n\n`candidate_59` deletes that entire correct pattern (removing\n`MainActivity.onDestroy()`, the `downloadCompletionReceiver` field, and the\nreceiver register/unregister calls) and replaces it with a brand-new\n`DownloadManagerInterface` class (initially at\n`.../data/DownloadManagerInterface.java`, later moved to\n`.../data/downloadmanager/DownloadManagerInterface.java` in candidate_63,\nmatching the fix's file path). This new class registers the same kind of\nbroadcast receiver in its `registerReceiver()` method (called from\n`init()`, invoked from `SebApp.onCreate()`), but no corresponding\n\"unregister\"/\"destroy\" method or call was added anywhere in this commit or\nany subsequent candidate until the fix commit itself. In other words,\ncandidate_59 both removed the previously-correct disposal logic and\nintroduced the new receiver registration that lacks disposal - exactly the\ndefect the fix (candidate_67 equivalent code) later corrects by adding\n`destroy(Context)` and wiring it into `MainActivity.onDestroy()`.\n\nLater candidates (e.g. candidate_60, which moves the `init()` call from\n`SebApp` into `MainActivity.onCreate()`, and candidate_63, which renames/\nrelocates the class) modify how and where the interface is initialized, but\nnone of them add the missing disposal logic - the receiver leak persists\nunchanged from candidate_59 through to the fix. Note: candidate_67 in this\ncandidate set is textually identical to the provided fix_commit_diff.txt,\nindicating it is the fix commit itself rather than a distinct\nbug-introducing candidate, so it was excluded from consideration.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
